# top-crim-03-01: Mistake of Fact vs Mistake of Law (10 MCQs: 061 to 070)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-061", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "The legal maxim 'Ignorantia facti excusat, ignorantia juris non excusat' means:", ["Ignorance of fact is no excuse, ignorance of law excuses", "Ignorance of fact excuses, ignorance of law does not excuse", "Both ignorance of fact and law excuse completely", "Neither ignorance of fact nor law can ever excuse in crime"], 1, "B", "Mistake of fact is a recognized defence under Sections 14 and 17 BNS / Sections 76 and 79 IPC, but mistake of law is never an excuse.", {"A": "Inverts the maxim.", "B": "Correct; ignorance of fact excuses, law does not.", "C": "Law never excuses.", "D": "Fact does excuse."}, "Easy", "BNS Sections 14 & 17 / IPC Sections 76 & 79", "Translate foundational mistake maxim."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-062", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "What is the key difference between Section 14 BNS (Section 76 IPC) and Section 17 BNS (Section 79 IPC)?", ["Section 14 relates to private citizens, Section 17 to judges", "Section 14 deals with belief of being BOUND by law; Section 17 deals with belief of being JUSTIFIED by law", "Section 14 excuses mistake of law; Section 17 excuses mistake of fact", "Section 14 applies to civil wrongs; Section 17 applies to crimes"], 1, "B", "Section 14 / S. 76 covers acts done by a person bound by law or believing in good faith to be bound by law; Section 17 / S. 79 covers acts justified by law or believed in good faith to be justified by law.", {"A": "Both apply across citizens and officials.", "B": "Correct; bound by law vs justified by law.", "C": "Neither excuses mistake of law.", "D": "Both are penal exceptions."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 14 vs Section 17", "Distinguish bound by law from justified by law."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-063", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "A, a soldier, fires on a mob by the order of his superior officer, in conformity with the commands of the law. A has committed:", ["Murder", "Culpable homicide", "No offence under Section 14 BNS / Section 76 IPC", "Abetment of riot"], 2, "C", "Under Section 14 BNS / Section 76 IPC (Illustration a), a soldier obeying lawful orders of a superior in conformity with law commits no offence.", {"A": "No offence under general exception.", "B": "Exception applies.", "C": "Correct; protected by Section 14 BNS / S. 76 IPC.", "D": "Lawful dispersal, not abetment."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 14 Illustration (a)", "Apply soldier obeying lawful command exception."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-064", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "A sees Z commit what appears to A to be a murder. A, in the exercise, to the best of his judgment exerted in good faith, of the power which the law gives to all persons of apprehending murderers in the act, seizes Z in order to bring him before the proper authorities. A has committed:", ["Wrongful restraint", "Assault", "No offence under Section 17 BNS / Section 79 IPC", "Attempt to kidnap"], 2, "C", "Under Section 17 BNS / Section 79 IPC (Illustration), A acts in good faith believing himself justified by law, even if Z was acting in self-defence.", {"A": "Protected by Section 17.", "B": "Protected by Section 17.", "C": "Correct; protected by belief of justification in law.", "D": "Lawful citizen apprehension."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 17 Illustration", "Apply justified by law mistake of fact illustration."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-065", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "In R v. Tolson (1889), the defence of mistake of fact succeeded in bigamy because:", ["The second marriage was conducted in a church", "Mrs. Tolson had formed a bona fide belief on reasonable grounds after 7 years of inquiry that her first husband was dead", "The statute explicitly excluded mens rea", "Bigamy is a civil wrong in England"], 1, "B", "The Court for Crown Cases Reserved held that an honest and reasonable belief in the existence of circumstances which, if true, would make the act innocent, is a good defence in crime.", {"A": "Ceremony location irrelevant.", "B": "Correct; bona fide belief on reasonable grounds of husband's death.", "C": "Mens rea was presumed.", "D": "Bigamy is a felony."}, "Hard", "(1889) 23 QBD 168", "Apply R v. Tolson mistake of fact precedent."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-066", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "An officer of a Court, being ordered by warrant from that Court to arrest Y, and after due enquiry believing Z to be Y, arrests Z. What offence has the officer committed?", ["Wrongful confinement", "Assault", "No offence under Section 14 BNS / Section 76 IPC", "Kidnapping"], 2, "C", "Under Illustration (b) to Section 76 IPC / Section 14 BNS, the officer, by reason of a mistake of fact and in good faith believing himself bound by law to arrest Z, commits no offence.", {"A": "Protected by Section 14.", "B": "Protected by Section 14.", "C": "Correct; mistake of fact under official warrant.", "D": "No offence."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 14 Illustration (b)", "Apply mistake of fact to arrest warrant execution."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-067", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "Can a foreigner arriving in India claim as a defence under Section 17 BNS / Section 79 IPC that they were ignorant of an Indian statutory regulation?", ["Yes, foreigners are entitled to presumption of ignorance", "No, ignorance of law cannot be pleaded by citizens or foreigners alike", "Yes, if they arrived less than 24 hours ago", "Yes, with permission of the embassy"], 1, "B", "In State of Maharashtra v. Mayer Hans George, the Supreme Court confirmed that ignorance of law provides no defence to any person within Indian territory.", {"A": "Foreigners have no special exemption.", "B": "Correct; ignorance of law is no defence.", "C": "Time of arrival irrelevant.", "D": "Embassy cannot grant criminal immunity."}, "Medium", "M.H. George, AIR 1965 SC 722", "Confirm universal bar on mistake of law."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-068", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "Under both Sections 14 and 17 BNS, the mistake of fact MUST be accompanied by:", ["Legal advice from a senior advocate", "'Good faith' (due care and attention under S. 2(11))", "Prior approval of a Judicial Magistrate", "Written witness statements"], 1, "B", "Sections 14 and 17 explicitly require that the person 'in good faith believes' themselves bound or justified by law. Good faith requires due care and attention.", {"A": "Advocate advice not statutory test.", "B": "Correct; good faith is mandatory.", "C": "No magistrate approval required.", "D": "No written statements required."}, "Easy", "BNS Sections 14 & 17", "Identify good faith as mandatory component."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-069", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "A shoots at what he believes in good faith to be a tiger in thick jungle, but it turns out to be a human trespasser B. If A acted with due care and caution, A is protected under:", ["Section 14 BNS", "Section 17 BNS / Section 79 IPC", "Section 106 BNS", "Section 303 BNS"], 1, "B", "In State of Orissa v. Ram Bahadur Thapa (1960), shooting under a bona fide mistake of fact believing the target to be a ghost or wild animal was held protected under Section 79 IPC / Section 17 BNS.", {"A": "Not bound by law.", "B": "Correct; justified by law under mistake of fact.", "C": "Rashness excluded by due care.", "D": "Theft irrelevant."}, "Hard", "AIR 1960 Ori 161", "Apply mistake of fact in hunting and wildlife scenarios."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-070", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-01", "If an act is prohibited by an illegal order of a military commander, can a subordinate soldier plead Section 14 BNS / Section 76 IPC if the order was manifestly illegal on its face?", ["Yes, a soldier must obey all orders blindly", "No; obedience to an order which is manifestly unlawful and contrary to law affords no protection", "Yes, if the commander confirms it in writing", "Yes, in times of war only"], 1, "B", "The order must be in 'conformity with the commands of the law'. A soldier who shoots innocent civilians under an obviously illegal order cannot claim Section 76 / 14.", {"A": "Blind obedience rejected in Nuremberg principles.", "B": "Correct; manifestly illegal orders afford no defence.", "C": "Writing cannot legalize unlawful acts.", "D": "War does not permit war crimes."}, "Hard", "BNS Section 14 Limits", "Analyze limits of superior orders in criminal law."))

# top-crim-03-02: Judicial Acts & Orders of Court (10 MCQs: 071 to 080)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-071", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "Under Section 15 BNS / Section 77 IPC, an act done by a Judge is exempt from criminal liability when acting:", ["In their personal residence", "Judicially in the exercise of any power which is, or which in good faith he believes to be, given to him by law", "In any administrative committee meeting", "After receiving sanction from the Chief Justice"], 1, "B", "Section 15 BNS / Section 77 IPC protects acts of a Judge when acting judicially in exercise of power given, or in good faith believed to be given, by law.", {"A": "Personal acts not protected.", "B": "Correct; acting judicially in good faith.", "C": "Must be acting judicially.", "D": "No CJ sanction requirement in S. 77."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 15 / IPC Section 77", "Master judicial acts criminal immunity."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-072", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "Under Section 16 BNS / Section 78 IPC, a hangman who executes a sentence of death passed by a Court commits no offence, even if:", ["The condemned prisoner was innocent", "The Court which passed the judgment had no jurisdiction to pass it, provided the hangman in good faith believed the Court had jurisdiction", "The warrant was unsigned", "The hangman acted with personal hatred"], 1, "B", "Section 16 BNS / Section 78 IPC explicitly protects ministerial officers executing court warrants even if the court had no jurisdiction, provided the officer in good faith believed jurisdiction existed.", {"A": "Innocence of prisoner doesn't affect executioner.", "B": "Correct; protected even if court lacked jurisdiction.", "C": "Defective warrant without seal is invalid.", "D": "Must act pursuant to order in good faith."}, "Hard", "BNS Section 16 / IPC Section 78", "Master execution of court orders exception."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-073", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "What is the primary rationale behind Section 15 BNS / Section 77 IPC judicial immunity?", ["To place judges above the law", "To ensure judicial independence so judges may administer justice fearlessly without apprehension of vexatious prosecutions", "To grant immunity to government servants generally", "To comply with international diplomatic treaties"], 1, "B", "Judicial independence under Article 50 demands that judges be insulated from criminal harassment for decisions rendered in good faith.", {"A": "Judges are not above the law in personal crime.", "B": "Correct; fearless and independent administration of justice.", "C": "Not general executive immunity.", "D": "Not diplomatic treaty."}, "Medium", "Judicial Protection Rationale", "Understand judicial independence in penal immunity."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-074", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "A Magistrate sentences an accused to 6 months imprisonment under a statute which had been repealed 2 days earlier, of which the Magistrate was unaware despite exercising reasonable diligence. Is the Magistrate criminally liable for wrongful confinement?", ["Yes, ignorance of law is no excuse", "No; protected under Section 15 BNS / Section 77 IPC as he acted judicially in good faith believing jurisdiction existed", "Yes, liable for compensation under civil law only", "Yes, liable to departmental censure"], 1, "B", "Section 15 BNS / Section 77 IPC protects a Judge who in good faith believes the power to be given to him by law.", {"A": "Judicial immunity extends to bona fide belief of law.", "B": "Correct; protected under Section 15 BNS / S. 77 IPC.", "C": "Judicial Officers Protection Act 1850 bars civil liability too.", "D": "Criminal liability is the question."}, "Hard", "BNS Section 15", "Apply judicial immunity to belief of statutory authority."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-075", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "Does Section 16 BNS / Section 78 IPC protect a police officer who executes a search warrant which has expired on its face?", ["Yes, police always enjoy immunity", "No; Section 16 protects acts done pursuant to a judgment or order whilst such order remains in force", "Yes, if the officer was busy", "Yes, if the magistrate later ratifies it"], 1, "B", "Section 16 protects acts done while the order remains in force. Executing an expired or cancelled warrant is unlawful.", {"A": "No blanket immunity.", "B": "Correct; order must be in force.", "C": "Administrative delay no excuse.", "D": "Ex-post ratification cannot legalize illegal search."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 16", "Identify limits of ministerial execution protection."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-076", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "A Judge who accepts a bribe to acquit an accused in a criminal case can claim:", ["Section 15 BNS / Section 77 IPC immunity because he was presiding in court", "No protection under Section 15 / 77 because bribery is not an act done in good faith or judicially", "Immunity until impeachment by Parliament", "Protection under Section 14 BNS"], 1, "B", "Accepting a bribe is a corrupt criminal act, not an act done judicially in good faith; judicial immunity is completely unavailable (K. Veeraswami v. Union of India).", {"A": "Immunity covers judicial acts, not corrupt crimes.", "B": "Correct; bribery destroys good faith and judicial character.", "C": "Impeachment is for removal; criminal trial proceeds after CJI sanction.", "D": "Section 14 irrelevant."}, "Easy", "K. Veeraswami v. Union of India, (1991) 3 SCC 655", "Demarcate corrupt acts from protected judicial acts."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-077", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "Under Section 16 BNS / Section 78 IPC, the officer executing the order of the court must show that:", ["He read the entire trial judgment", "He acted in good faith believing that the Court had jurisdiction to pass the order", "The order was confirmed by the Supreme Court", "The accused consented to the execution"], 1, "B", "The statutory condition in Section 16 BNS / Section 78 IPC is that the person executing the order in good faith believes that the Court had such jurisdiction.", {"A": "Executioner need not read full judgment.", "B": "Correct; good faith belief in court jurisdiction.", "C": "No SC confirmation needed.", "D": "Consent not required."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 16", "Recall statutory condition for warrant execution."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-078", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "A bailiff enters B's house with a valid civil warrant of attachment issued by a competent court. B resists and uses force. Is the bailiff guilty of criminal trespass?", ["Yes, if B protested", "No, protected under Section 16 BNS / Section 78 IPC", "Yes, civil court orders do not protect against criminal trespass", "Yes, if property belonged to third party"], 1, "B", "The bailiff is executing an order of a court of justice in force and is fully protected under Section 16 BNS / Section 78 IPC.", {"A": "Protest does not invalidate legal process.", "B": "Correct; protected by Section 16 BNS / Section 78 IPC.", "C": "Orders of civil courts are fully covered.", "D": "Bona fide execution under warrant is protected."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 16", "Apply court order protection to civil execution bailiffs."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-079", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "Which statute works alongside Section 15 BNS / Section 77 IPC to provide comprehensive protection to judicial officers?", ["Judicial Officers Protection Act, 1850 & Judges (Protection) Act, 1985", "Advocates Act, 1961", "Police Act, 1861", "Armed Forces Special Powers Act"], 0, "A", "The Judicial Officers Protection Act, 1850 (civil immunity) and Judges (Protection) Act, 1985 (bar on criminal/civil proceedings) complement Section 77 IPC / Section 15 BNS.", {"A": "Correct; 1850 and 1985 Acts.", "B": "Advocates Act regulates bar.", "C": "Police Act regulates police.", "D": "AFSPA is military."}, "Medium", "Judicial Protection Legislation", "Identify statutory framework protecting judicial officers."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-080", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-02", "If a court issues a warrant directing arrest of A, but the constable intentionally arrests B because of personal rivalry, can the constable plead Section 16 BNS?", ["Yes, constable held a warrant", "No; the constable did not act in good faith pursuant to the warrant", "Yes, constable can rectify the mistake later", "Yes, with permission of the SP"], 1, "B", "Intentional arrest of a different person out of personal rivalry completely lacks good faith and violates the warrant; Section 16 affords zero protection.", {"A": "Warrant did not name B.", "B": "Correct; lack of good faith and intentional malice excludes protection.", "C": "Subsequent release does not wipe criminal liability.", "D": "SP cannot legalize illegal arrest."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 16 Limits", "Distinguish intentional malice from protected execution."))

# top-crim-03-03: Accident, Necessity & Infancy (10 MCQs: 081 to 090)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-081", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "Under Section 18 BNS / Section 80 IPC (Accident), which of the following is NOT an essential ingredient?", ["Done by accident or misfortune", "Done without any criminal intention or knowledge", "Done in the doing of a lawful act in a lawful manner by lawful means", "Done after obtaining prior permission of police authorities"], 3, "D", "Police permission is not an element of Section 18 BNS / Section 80 IPC. The ingredients are: accident, no criminal intention/knowledge, lawful act, lawful manner/means, with proper care and caution.", {"A": "Essential ingredient.", "B": "Essential ingredient.", "C": "Essential ingredient.", "D": "Correct; police permission is not an ingredient."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 18 / IPC Section 80", "Identify statutory ingredients of Accident defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-082", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "A is at work with a hatchet; the head flies off and kills a man who is standing by. If there was no want of proper caution on the part of A, A's act is:", ["Excusable as an accident under Section 18 BNS / Section 80 IPC", "Culpable homicide not amounting to murder", "Negligent homicide under Section 106 BNS", "Grievous hurt"], 0, "A", "Under Section 18 BNS / Section 80 IPC (Illustration), A's act is excusable and not an offence because it was a lawful act done in a lawful manner with proper care and caution.", {"A": "Correct; classic accident illustration.", "B": "No criminal intention or knowledge.", "C": "No want of caution, so negligence excluded.", "D": "Accident excludes hurt offences."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 18 Illustration", "Apply classic hatchet accident illustration."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-083", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "The maxim 'Quod necessitas non habet legem' forms the philosophical foundation of:", ["Section 18 BNS (Accident)", "Section 19 BNS / Section 81 IPC (Necessity)", "Section 20 BNS (Infancy)", "Section 22 BNS (Insanity)"], 1, "B", "The maxim means 'Necessity knows no law' and underpins the defence of Necessity in Section 19 BNS / Section 81 IPC.", {"A": "Accident is misfortune.", "B": "Correct; Necessity knows no law.", "C": "Infancy is doli incapax.", "D": "Insanity is non compos mentis."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 19 / IPC Section 81", "Match legal maxim with Necessity defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-084", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "In the landmark English authority R v. Dudley and Stephens (1884), the defence of necessity was rejected because:", ["Cannibalism is a civil wrong", "Self-preservation does not justify the deliberate killing of an innocent person to save one's own life", "The ship was registered in Australia", "The cabin boy had consented"], 1, "B", "Lord Coleridge C.J. held that necessity can never justify taking the life of an innocent person to preserve one's own life.", {"A": "Cannibalism is murder.", "B": "Correct; self-preservation cannot justify murder.", "C": "Jurisdiction was established.", "D": "Consent to death is no defence under law."}, "Hard", "(1884) 14 QBD 273", "Apply R v. Dudley and Stephens necessity doctrine."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-085", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "A, the captain of a vessel, suddenly finds himself in such a position that before he can stop, he must inevitably run down a boat B with 30 passengers, unless he alters course, in which case he must run down boat C with 2 passengers. A alters course and runs down boat C in good faith to save 30 passengers. A is protected under:", ["Section 18 BNS (Accident)", "Section 19 BNS / Section 81 IPC (Necessity)", "Section 34 BNS (Self-defence)", "Section 33 BNS (Trivial acts)"], 1, "B", "Under Section 19 BNS / Section 81 IPC (Illustration), A is not guilty of an offence if he acted without criminal intention and in good faith to avoid greater harm to the 30 passengers.", {"A": "Deliberate navigation, not accident.", "B": "Correct; prevention of greater harm under necessity.", "C": "Not self-defence against attack.", "D": "Loss of life is not trivial."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 19 Illustration", "Apply vessel collision necessity illustration."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-086", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "The doctrine of 'Doli Incapax' (incapable of committing crime) is codified under Section 20 BNS / Section 82 IPC for a child under the age of:", ["Five years", "Seven years", "Twelve years", "Eighteen years"], 1, "B", "Section 20 BNS / Section 82 IPC enacts an absolute irrebuttable presumption: 'Nothing is an offence which is done by a child under seven years of age.'", {"A": "Historic Roman law limit.", "B": "Correct; strictly under seven years of age.", "C": "7 to 12 is rebuttable under Section 21.", "D": "Juvenile Justice Act covers minors under 18."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 20 / IPC Section 82", "Recall the age limit for absolute Doli Incapax."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-087", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "Under Section 21 BNS / Section 83 IPC, a child above seven years and under twelve years of age is immune from criminal liability ONLY IF the child:", ["Belongs to a low-income family", "Has not attained sufficient maturity of understanding to judge the nature and consequences of his conduct on that occasion", "Did not use a deadly weapon", "Acted under direction of his parents"], 1, "B", "Section 21 BNS / Section 83 IPC establishes a rebuttable presumption of doli incapax; liability depends on whether the child has attained sufficient maturity of understanding.", {"A": "Income irrelevant.", "B": "Correct; immature understanding is the statutory test.", "C": "Weapon used may be evidence of maturity.", "D": "Parental command is not the test."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 21 / IPC Section 83", "Master rebuttable infancy presumption."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-088", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "A 10-year-old child picks up a gold necklace from a shop, immediately conceals it in his shoe, and sells it to a jeweller for cash. Is the child protected under Section 21 BNS / Section 83 IPC?", ["Yes, every child under 12 has absolute immunity", "No; hiding the necklace in the shoe and selling it demonstrates sufficient maturity of understanding", "Yes, provided the parents return the money", "Yes, child can never be prosecuted under any law"], 1, "B", "In Ulla Mahapatra (1950), conduct such as concealing the stolen property or weapon was held to demonstrate mature understanding, defeating the Section 83 / 21 defence.", {"A": "7 to 12 is rebuttable, not absolute.", "B": "Correct; conduct exhibits maturity of understanding.", "C": "Restitution does not wipe crime.", "D": "Subject to Juvenile Justice Act procedure."}, "Hard", "Ulla Mahapatra, 1950 Cri LJ 399", "Apply maturity of understanding test in children."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-089", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "What type of presumption is enacted under Section 20 BNS / Section 82 IPC regarding a 6-year-old child?", ["Rebuttable presumption of law", "Conclusive and irrebuttable presumption of law", "Presumption of fact under Section 114 Evidence Act", "Discretionary judicial presumption"], 1, "B", "The presumption that a child under 7 years cannot commit a crime is conclusive and irrebuttable; no evidence can be led by prosecution to show the child knew the act was wrong.", {"A": "Rebuttable applies to 7–12 years.", "B": "Correct; conclusive, irrebuttable presumption of law.", "C": "Not presumption of fact.", "D": "Mandatory, not discretionary."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 20 / Evidence Act Section 4", "Classify infancy presumption type."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-03-090", "ch-crim-03", "top-crim-03-03", "In Bishambhar v. Roomal (1951), villagers blackened the face of a molester and paraded him on a donkey to prevent caste rioting. The defence of necessity under Section 81 IPC failed because:", ["Caste disputes are not cognizable", "Harm caused was intentionally inflicted directly upon an individual, which cannot be justified under Section 81", "Section 81 applies only to fires and shipwrecks", "The police were not informed"], 1, "B", "Section 81 protects acts done without criminal intent to avoid greater harm, but does not legalize the intentional commission of an offence against an individual as private vigilante punishment.", {"A": "Irrelevant.", "B": "Correct; intentional private punishment not covered by necessity.", "C": "Section 81 is general, not restricted to ships.", "D": "Police notice not the core holding."}, "Hard", "AIR 1951 All 500", "Understand limits of necessity against private vengeance."))

