# top-crim-04-01: Insanity & Intoxication (10 MCQs: 091 to 100)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-091", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "Section 22 BNS / Section 84 IPC is founded upon which celebrated English common law rules?", ["The Durham Rule", "The M'Naghten Rules (1843)", "The Irresistible Impulse Doctrine", "The American Model Penal Code Rule"], 1, "B", "Section 22 BNS / Section 84 IPC codifies the classic M'Naghten Rules formulated by the House of Lords in 1843.", {"A": "Product test rejected in India.", "B": "Correct; M'Naghten Rules (1843).", "C": "Irresistible impulse is not recognized under S. 84.", "D": "American MPC is distinct."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 22 / IPC Section 84", "Identify common law source of Indian insanity defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-092", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "What is the crucial statutory standard for legal insanity under Section 22 BNS / Section 84 IPC?", ["Mental depression and psychological mood swings", "Incapable of knowing the nature of the act, OR that it is either wrong or contrary to law, by reason of unsoundness of mind", "Subnormal IQ certified by a psychologist", "Irresistible impulse driving the accused to commit murder"], 1, "B", "The cognitive impairment must be such that the person is incapable of knowing the nature of the act, or that it is wrong or contrary to law.", {"A": "Medical depression is not legal insanity.", "B": "Correct; statutory cognitive incapacity test.", "C": "Low IQ is not legal insanity.", "D": "Irresistible impulse does not excuse in India."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 22 / IPC Section 84", "Recall statutory test for legal insanity."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-093", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "What is the fundamental distinction between 'Medical Insanity' and 'Legal Insanity' (Surendra Mishra v. State of Jharkhand)?", ["Medical insanity is temporary, legal insanity is permanent", "Every person who is mentally ill (medical insanity) is not legally insane; legal insanity requires total cognitive incapacity at the time of committing the act", "Medical insanity excuses crime, legal insanity does not", "Legal insanity is determined by doctors, medical insanity by judges"], 1, "B", "Medical insanity covers all psychological disorders, but legal insanity under Section 84 requires cognitive incapacity to know right from wrong at the exact time of the offence.", {"A": "Duration is not the test.", "B": "Correct; legal insanity requires cognitive destruction at time of act.", "C": "Only legal insanity excuses crime.", "D": "Judges determine legal insanity based on law."}, "Hard", "(2011) 11 SCC 497", "Distinguish legal insanity from medical insanity."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-094", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "Under Section 105 Evidence Act / Section 108 BSA, upon whom does the burden of proving legal insanity rest?", ["Upon the prosecution beyond reasonable doubt", "Upon the accused, by a preponderance of probabilities", "Upon the medical board appointed by court", "Upon the victim"], 1, "B", "The court presumes the absence of circumstances bringing the case within general exceptions. The burden of proving insanity is on the accused, dischargeable on a preponderance of probabilities (Dahyabhai).", {"A": "Prosecution proves guilt, not general exceptions.", "B": "Correct; burden on accused on preponderance of probability.", "C": "Medical board is expert evidence.", "D": "Victim has no burden."}, "Medium", "Dahyabhai v. State of Gujarat, AIR 1964 SC 1563", "Master burden of proof in insanity defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-095", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "Under Section 23 BNS / Section 85 IPC, involuntary intoxication is a complete defence ONLY IF the thing which intoxicated the person was administered:", ["By a public servant", "Without his knowledge or against his will", "In an alcoholic beverage at a social party", "While the person was sleeping"], 1, "B", "Section 23 BNS / Section 85 IPC explicitly requires that the intoxicating substance was administered to the person 'without his knowledge or against his will'.", {"A": "Public servant status irrelevant.", "B": "Correct; without knowledge or against will.", "C": "Voluntary drinking at parties is not involuntary.", "D": "Sleeping administration is only one example of lack of knowledge."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 23 / IPC Section 85", "Identify core element of involuntary intoxication."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-096", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "In Basdev v. State of Pepsu (1956), concerning voluntary intoxication under Section 86 IPC / Section 24 BNS, the Supreme Court held that:", ["Voluntary intoxication completely excuses murder", "While knowledge is attributed to the drunken accused as if he were sober, intention cannot be automatically presumed and must be gathered from surrounding facts", "A drunken person is presumed to have intended every consequence", "Voluntary drunkenness attracts capital punishment"], 1, "B", "Under Section 86, the law presumes knowledge on par with a sober man, but intention must be inferred from surrounding circumstances and cannot be blindly imputed.", {"A": "Voluntary drunkenness never excuses.", "B": "Correct; knowledge is presumed, intention is gathered from facts.", "C": "Overstates presumption.", "D": "Sentence depends on charge."}, "Hard", "AIR 1956 SC 488", "Master Basdev doctrine on voluntary intoxication."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-097", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "A gets voluntarily intoxicated at a wedding. When requested to step aside by a boy B, A draws a pistol, says 'I will shoot you if you don't move', and shoots B dead. A's defence of voluntary intoxication will:", ["Succeed under Section 23 BNS", "Fail, because drawing a pistol and uttering threats demonstrates clear intention and knowledge under Basdev principles", "Reduce the offence to simple hurt", "Result in community service"], 1, "B", "The facts are directly identical to Basdev v. State of Pepsu; drawing a weapon and uttering threats showed that A's mind was capable of forming the intention to shoot.", {"A": "Voluntary, not involuntary.", "B": "Correct; intention and knowledge demonstrated.", "C": "Fatal gunshot is not simple hurt.", "D": "Murder cannot receive community service."}, "Hard", "Basdev v. State of Pepsu, AIR 1956 SC 488", "Apply voluntary intoxication principles to shooting incident."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-098", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "Does the defence of 'Irresistible Impulse' excuse a crime under Section 22 BNS / Section 84 IPC?", ["Yes, mental impulse is recognized in Section 84", "No; Section 84 requires cognitive incapacity; emotional or uncontrollable impulse where the accused knows the act is wrong affords no legal defence", "Yes, in crimes of passion", "Yes, if certified by a psychiatrist"], 1, "B", "Indian law strictly adheres to the cognitive test of M'Naghten; irresistible impulse where the actor knows the act is wrong does not satisfy Section 84.", {"A": "Impulse is not cognitive incapacity.", "B": "Correct; irresistible impulse does not excuse under Indian law.", "C": "Crimes of passion may at best attract Exception 1 to S. 101/300.", "D": "Medical opinion cannot rewrite statute."}, "Medium", "State of M.P. v. Ahmadulla, AIR 1961 SC 998", "Confirm rejection of irresistible impulse in Indian law."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-099", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "Delirium Tremens (temporary insanity caused by habitual long-term drinking) is treated under criminal law as:", ["Voluntary intoxication under Section 86", "Unsoundness of mind (insanity) under Section 84 / Section 22 BNS", "Strict liability crime", "No defence under any section"], 1, "B", "Under settled law, madness or delirium tremens produced by drunkenness is treated as ordinary insanity under Section 84 / 22, not as voluntary intoxication.", {"A": "Not treated as voluntary drunkenness.", "B": "Correct; treated as insanity under Section 84 / 22 BNS.", "C": "Not strict liability.", "D": "Recognized defence."}, "Hard", "R v. Davis, (1881) 14 Cox CC 563", "Classify Delirium Tremens in criminal defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-100", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-01", "At what precise moment must the unsoundness of mind exist for Section 22 BNS / Section 84 IPC to apply?", ["At the time of trial", "At the time of committing the act", "At the time of arrest by police", "Throughout the preceding 6 months"], 1, "B", "The statutory phrase is: 'who, at the time of doing it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is incapable of knowing...'. Antecedent or subsequent insanity is only relevant to infer state of mind at the time of the act.", {"A": "Unsoundness at trial leads to inquiry under BNSS 367.", "B": "Correct; at the exact time of doing the act.", "C": "Arrest state irrelevant to offence.", "D": "Prior history is evidential, not conclusive."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 22 text", "Pinpoint the crucial timing for insanity defence."))

# top-crim-04-02: Consent, Trivial Acts & Duress (10 MCQs: 101 to 110)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-101", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "The common law maxim 'Volenti non fit injuria' means:", ["An act done under necessity is lawful", "No injury is done to one who consents", "Ignorance of law does not excuse", "The King can do no wrong"], 1, "B", "Volenti non fit injuria underpins the consent exceptions in Sections 25 to 29 BNS / Sections 87 to 91 IPC.", {"A": "Quod necessitas non habet legem.", "B": "Correct; harm suffered with consent is not legal injury.", "C": "Ignorantia juris.", "D": "Sovereign immunity."}, "Easy", "BNS Sections 25–29 / IPC Sections 87–91", "Translate Volenti non fit injuria."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-102", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "Under Section 28 BNS / Section 90 IPC, consent is NOT valid if given:", ["By a person above 18 years of age", "Under fear of injury, or misconception of fact, or by a person of unsound mind, intoxicated, or under twelve years of age", "In writing before a notary", "In commercial transactions"], 1, "B", "Section 28 BNS / Section 90 IPC defines vitiated consent: fear of injury, misconception of fact, child under 12 years, or person of unsound mind / intoxicated.", {"A": "Valid consent.", "B": "Correct; fear, misconception, child <12, intoxication/insanity.", "C": "Notary does not vitiate.", "D": "Commercial consent valid."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 28 / IPC Section 90", "Identify grounds vitiating consent in penal law."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-103", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "Under Section 25 BNS / Section 87 IPC (harm consented in sports), consent cannot justify an act which is intended or known to be likely to cause:", ["Simple hurt", "Death or grievous hurt", "Damage to sports equipment", "Temporary bruise"], 1, "B", "Section 25 BNS / Section 87 IPC applies only to acts not intended and not known to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt (e.g. fencing, cricket).", {"A": "Simple hurt is covered by sport consent.", "B": "Correct; cannot consent to death or grievous hurt under S. 87.", "C": "Equipment damage not penal.", "D": "Bruises covered by sport consent."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 25 / IPC Section 87", "Recall limits of consent in games and sports."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-104", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "Under Section 32 BNS / Section 94 IPC (Duress / Compulsion), which two offences are STRICTLY EXCLUDED and can NEVER be excused by threats of instant death?", ["Murder and offences against the State punishable with death", "Theft and house-breaking", "Kidnapping and extortion", "Forgery and perjury"], 0, "A", "Section 32 BNS / Section 94 IPC explicitly excludes Murder and offences against the State punishable with death (e.g. waging war).", {"A": "Correct; murder and capital state offences are never excused by duress.", "B": "Property crimes can be excused by duress.", "C": "Extortion/kidnapping can be excused if compelled.", "D": "Document crimes can be excused."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 32 / IPC Section 94", "Identify offences excluded from duress defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-105", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "A is seized by a gang of dacoits and threatened with instant death if he does not force open the door of a house with his blacksmith tools. A opens the door. What offence has A committed?", ["Dacoity with murder", "House-breaking by night", "No offence under Section 32 BNS / Section 94 IPC", "Abetment of dacoity"], 2, "C", "Under Illustration (a) to Section 94 IPC / Section 32 BNS, a blacksmith forced by threats of instant death commits no offence as he did not place himself in that position voluntarily.", {"A": "Protected by duress.", "B": "Protected by duress.", "C": "Correct; protected by Section 32 BNS / S. 94 IPC.", "D": "No abetment under compulsion."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 32 Illustration (a)", "Apply blacksmith duress illustration."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-106", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "Under Section 33 BNS / Section 95 IPC, the maxim 'De minimis non curat lex' means:", ["The law does not concern itself with trifles", "Ignorance of fact excuses", "No crime without an act", "Every injury must be compensated"], 0, "A", "De minimis non curat lex means the law does not take account of trifles; harm so slight that no person of ordinary sense and temper would complain is excused.", {"A": "Correct; law ignores trifles.", "B": "Ignorantia facti.", "C": "Actus reus.", "D": "Tort principle."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 33 / IPC Section 95", "Translate De minimis non curat lex."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-107", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "A accidentally brushes his elbow against B while boarding a crowded Mumbai local train, causing minor contact. B files a complaint of criminal force against A. The court will dismiss the complaint under:", ["Section 18 BNS (Accident)", "Section 33 BNS / Section 95 IPC (Act causing slight harm / De minimis)", "Section 34 BNS (Private defence)", "Section 20 BNS (Infancy)"], 1, "B", "Brushing past in crowded public transit is a classic trifle of daily life protected under Section 95 IPC / Section 33 BNS.", {"A": "Misfortune not needed when trivial.", "B": "Correct; trivial harm under Section 33 BNS / Section 95 IPC.", "C": "No self-defence needed.", "D": "A is adult."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 33 / IPC Section 95", "Apply trivial acts exception to crowded transit contact."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-108", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "Under Section 27 BNS / Section 89 IPC, who can give consent for an act done in good faith for the benefit of a child under twelve years of age or person of unsound mind?", ["The child himself if intelligent", "The guardian or other person having lawful charge of such person", "The local police officer", "Any municipal council member"], 1, "B", "Section 27 BNS / Section 89 IPC allows consent to be given by or on behalf of the child or person of unsound mind by their guardian or lawful custodian.", {"A": "Child <12 cannot consent.", "B": "Correct; guardian or person having lawful charge.", "C": "Police have no parental guardianship.", "D": "Municipal council has no standing."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 27 / IPC Section 89", "Identify who consents for minors under 12."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-109", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "A, a surgeon, seeing a child suffer an accident which is likely to prove fatal unless an operation be immediately performed, performs the operation without waiting to recover the guardian's consent. The child dies during operation. A is protected under:", ["Section 26 BNS", "Section 29 BNS / Section 92 IPC", "Section 18 BNS", "Section 32 BNS"], 1, "B", "Under Section 29 BNS / Section 92 IPC (Illustration c), performing an emergency surgical operation in good faith for a patient's benefit without consent when consent cannot be obtained in time is protected.", {"A": "Requires consent.", "B": "Correct; emergency act without consent under Section 29 BNS / S. 92 IPC.", "C": "Operation is intentional act, not accident.", "D": "No duress."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 29 / IPC Section 92 Illus. (c)", "Apply emergency medical intervention without consent."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-110", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-02", "If a person voluntarily joins a gang of dacoits knowing their character, can he afterwards plead duress under Section 32 BNS / Section 94 IPC when compelled by the gang to commit robbery?", ["Yes, threats of death always excuse", "No; Explanation 1 to Section 32 BNS / Section 94 IPC explicitly bars a person who voluntarily placed himself in such situation from pleading duress", "Yes, if he was unarmed", "Yes, if he was a minor"], 1, "B", "Explanation 1 to Section 94 IPC / Section 32 BNS provides that a person who, of his own accord, or by reason of a threat of being beaten, joins a gang of dacoits, cannot take the benefit of this exception.", {"A": "Self-induced duress is no defence.", "B": "Correct; voluntary gang membership excludes duress defence.", "C": "Weapons irrelevant.", "D": "Adult gang member."}, "Hard", "BNS Section 32 Explanation 1", "Analyze self-induced duress exclusion."))

# top-crim-04-03: Right of Private Defence of Body & Property (10 MCQs: 111 to 120)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-111", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "The right of private defence under Sections 34 to 44 BNS / Sections 96 to 106 IPC is:", ["A punitive right to punish criminals", "A preventive and protective right to defend oneself and others from unlawful aggression", "A retributive right of revenge", "An offensive right to eliminate enemies"], 1, "B", "Private defence is strictly defensive and preventive; it is not a right of retribution, punishment, or revenge.", {"A": "Punishment is exclusive state monopoly.", "B": "Correct; preventive and protective right.", "C": "Revenge is prohibited.", "D": "Offensive aggression is criminal."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 34 / IPC Section 96", "Define the legal character of private defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-112", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "Under Section 37 BNS / Section 99 IPC, there is NO right of private defence against an act done by a public servant acting in good faith under colour of his office, UNLESS the act causes reasonable apprehension of:", ["Wrongful arrest", "Death or grievous hurt", "Damage to reputation", "Financial loss"], 1, "B", "Section 37 BNS / Section 99 IPC provides that there is no right of private defence against a public servant unless the act causes reasonable apprehension of death or grievous hurt.", {"A": "Wrongful arrest does not justify violent defence.", "B": "Correct; death or grievous hurt apprehension restores defence.", "C": "Reputation not grounds for violent resistance.", "D": "Financial loss does not justify physical resistance."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 37 / IPC Section 99", "Identify when private defence lies against public servants."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-113", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "Under Section 37(3) BNS / Section 99(3) IPC, there is NO right of private defence in cases in which there is:", ["No weapon available", "Time to have recourse to the protection of the public authorities", "A large crowd witnessing the event", "Only one aggressor"], 1, "B", "Where there is reasonable opportunity and time to have recourse to the protection of the public authorities (police/magistrate), the right of private defence does not arise.", {"A": "Weapons not test.", "B": "Correct; recourse to public authorities bars private defence.", "C": "Crowd presence irrelevant.", "D": "Aggressor count irrelevant."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 37(3) / IPC Section 99(3)", "Recall the recourse to public authorities limitation."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-114", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "How much harm may be inflicted in the exercise of the right of private defence under Section 37(4) BNS / Section 99(4) IPC?", ["Any amount of harm including death in all cases", "No more harm than it is necessary to inflict for the purpose of defence (Proportionality)", "Equal harm to that suffered", "Double the harm attempted"], 1, "B", "Section 37(4) BNS / Section 99(4) IPC enacts the rule of proportionality: the right of private defence in no case extends to the inflicting of more harm than it is necessary to inflict for defence.", {"A": "Overstates right.", "B": "Correct; proportionality test: no more harm than necessary.", "C": "Lex talionis not test.", "D": "Vengeance not permitted."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 37(4) / IPC Section 99(4)", "Master proportionality rule in private defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-115", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "Under Section 38 BNS / Section 100 IPC, which of the following assaults justifies causing the DEATH of the assailant in private defence of body?", ["Assault with a small stick on the arm", "An assault with the intention of committing rape", "An assault causing simple hurt", "Threat of verbal abuse"], 1, "B", "Section 38 BNS / Section 100 IPC specifies grounds where death may be caused, including assault with intention of committing rape, unnatural lust, kidnapping/abduction, or acid attack.", {"A": "Stick bruise does not justify death.", "B": "Correct; assault with intention of committing rape justifies causing death.", "C": "Simple hurt does not justify death.", "D": "Verbal abuse never justifies death."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 38 / IPC Section 100", "Identify grounds where causing death is justified in body defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-116", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "Which ground for causing death in private defence of the body was added to Section 100 IPC in 2013 and preserved in Section 38 BNS?", ["Cyber stalking", "An act of throwing or administering acid, or an attempt to do so", "Pickpocketing on railway platforms", "Adultery"], 1, "B", "Clause 'Seventhly' was added by Criminal Law Amendment Act 2013: an act of throwing or administering acid or an attempt to throw acid causing apprehension of grievous hurt justifies causing death.", {"A": "Stalking not capital ground.", "B": "Correct; acid attack clause justifies causing death.", "C": "Theft does not justify death.", "D": "Adultery decriminalized."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 38(g) / IPC Section 100 Seventhly", "Recall 2013 acid attack amendment in Section 100/38."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-117", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "Under Section 41 BNS / Section 103 IPC, in which property offence does the right of private defence of property extend to causing the DEATH of the wrongdoer?", ["Theft of cash from a shop during daytime", "Robbery", "Mischief by killing a cow", "Criminal breach of trust"], 1, "B", "Under Section 41 BNS / Section 103 IPC, private defence of property extends to causing death in: Robbery, House-breaking by night, Mischief by fire to human dwelling/tent/vessel, or Theft/Mischief/House-trespass causing apprehension of death/grievous hurt.", {"A": "Simple daytime theft does not justify death.", "B": "Correct; Robbery is an express statutory ground.", "C": "Killing cattle is simple mischief.", "D": "Breach of trust is non-violent."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 41 / IPC Section 103", "Identify property offences where causing death is justified."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-118", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "When does the right of private defence of the body COMMENCE and how long does it CONTINUE under Section 39 BNS / Section 102 IPC?", ["Commences when actual blow is struck; continues until police arrive", "Commences as soon as reasonable apprehension of danger to body arises from attempt or threat; continues as long as such apprehension continues", "Commences only after filing an FIR; continues during trial", "Commences upon verbal challenge; continues permanently"], 1, "B", "Section 39 BNS / Section 102 IPC provides that the right commences as soon as a reasonable apprehension of danger arises from an attempt or threat, though the offence may not have been committed; and continues as long as apprehension continues.", {"A": "Victim need not wait for first blow.", "B": "Correct; reasonable apprehension starts and bounds the right.", "C": "FIR irrelevant to immediate defence.", "D": "Ends once danger ceases."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 39 / IPC Section 102", "Master duration and commencement of private defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-119", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "A is attacked by a mob who attempt to murder him. He cannot effectually exercise his right of private defence without firing on the mob, and he cannot fire without risk of harming innocent young children who are mingled with the mob. If A fires and kills an innocent child, A has committed:", ["Murder under Section 103 BNS", "Culpable homicide", "No offence under Section 44 BNS / Section 106 IPC", "Negligent homicide under Section 106 BNS"], 2, "C", "Section 44 BNS / Section 106 IPC explicitly provides that if in exercise of private defence against deadly assault the defender is so situated that he cannot exercise it without risk of harm to an innocent person, his right extends to running that risk.", {"A": "Protected by Section 44 BNS / S. 106 IPC.", "B": "Protected by Section 44.", "C": "Correct; classic innocent bystander risk illustration.", "D": "Not negligent; statutorily justified."}, "Hard", "BNS Section 44 / IPC Section 106", "Apply innocent bystander risk in private defence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-04-120", "ch-crim-04", "top-crim-04-03", "Under Section 41 BNS / Section 103 IPC, does the right to cause death in defence of property extend to 'House-breaking by DAY'?", ["Yes, house-breaking at any time justifies causing death", "No; Section 41 BNS / Section 103 IPC specifically restricts this right to 'House-breaking by NIGHT'", "Yes, if the house-breaker is armed", "Yes, if the house contains gold"], 1, "B", "Section 41 BNS / Section 103 IPC specifies 'Secondly — House-breaking by night'. Simple daytime house-breaking does NOT extend to causing death unless it creates apprehension of death or grievous hurt under clause Fourthly.", {"A": "Distinction between day and night is vital.", "B": "Correct; specifically house-breaking by night.", "C": "Unless weapon causes apprehension of death.", "D": "Contents irrelevant."}, "Hard", "BNS Section 41 / IPC Section 103", "Distinguish daytime from nighttime house-breaking in private defence."))

